{
  "id": "COMPOUND:42472 - PROTEIN:6152",
  "verbalization": "N-Methyltyramine is implicated in the pathway leading to Thioredoxin, mitochondrial, through a series of target relationships. The evidence suggests that N-Methyltyramine acts as a target of multiple enzymes in the Dehydrogenase/reductase SDR family, including SDR9, J, and Phorone, all of which are ultimately targeted by Thioredoxin, mitochondrial. This indicates a potential mechanism where N-Methyltyramine may influence the activity of these enzymes, which in turn affect Thioredoxin, mitochondrial. The recurring involvement of the SDR family and the shared target of Thioredoxin, mitochondrial suggests a possible regulatory or metabolic interaction. The evidence is consistent across three distinct paths, reinforcing the notion that N-Methyltyramine may modulate the activity of these enzymes, which could then influence the function of Thioredoxin, mitochondrial. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential role for N-Methyltyramine in modulating mitochondrial function through the SDR family of enzymes.",
